f 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486258 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "399a1def-da95-5c7b-b07c-dd77153c4399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486410 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186486410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e6a690a-1af9-54cd-96d8-6699f35c43cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486410 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- State